Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["ABM Project Plan", "Title",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977663556694519*^9, 3.977663562098715*^9}, {
  3.97827208844075*^9, 
  3.9782720924858856`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"12e3845f-e16d-fc4b-897f-\
273abfdd0040"],

Cell["\<\
Simulating the Emergence of Evolutionarily Stable Reproductive and Parenting \
Strategies using Agent-Based Modeling with Evolutionary Game Theory.\
\>", "Subtitle",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977664366254837*^9, 3.977664368599169*^9}, {
  3.9776888162938347`*^9, 3.977688862637703*^9}, {3.97769021182061*^9, 
  3.9776902333732376`*^9}, {3.977691230462988*^9, 3.9776912387393837`*^9}, {
  3.9776940850459385`*^9, 3.9776940885389347`*^9}, {3.9777473105752296`*^9, 
  3.977747317072031*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9a5b0013-8b30-284a-9cc8-\
f6a8808a4c22"],

Cell["Yash Juneja, Om Kalyani, Shreraj Sainani", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977663676013542*^9, 
  3.9776636830652256`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4580851f-af58-df4d-bf5c-\
75644240a4d6"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Problem and Group Fit", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977663579131777*^9, 3.9776635819321575`*^9}, {
  3.9777483753657017`*^9, 
  3.9777483764946823`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"be1b8abb-a084-5f43-b189-\
787da0f294ea"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\tThe processes by which life interacts with its environment, sustains \
itself, and continues on through its posterity (reproduces) fascinates us. As \
a group, we have found the emergence of evolutionary behaviors and traits of \
organisms through the process of natural selection interesting. We were \
particularly inspired by the ",
 StyleBox["Primer",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " YouTube channel: https://www.youtube.com/@PrimerBlobs for their insightful \
investigations of evolutionary phenomena done through Agent-Based Modeling \
simulations, ranging from behavior to genetics. Agent-Based Modeling (ABM) \
simulations, while greatly simplified, can provide interesting theory that \
can provide a basis and a framework for actual studies of ecological \
behaviors and traits in organisms. These simulations consist of agents that \
interact with each other and their environment through simple rules. As the \
simulation evolves through time, the rules produce interesting behavior among \
the agents that can be studied. These rules ideally mimic real life to create \
a realistic, albeit simplified, simulation.\n\tIn an ecological simulations \
context, ABM simulations can explain the emergence of  ecological phenomena \
by analyzing the behavior of agents over time based on their interactions and \
feedback for their environment. ",
 StyleBox["Primer",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " for example, explores the evolution of behaviors like teamwork or the \
development of traits of species through simulated ecosystems, in which \
simulated natural selection drives the process of the of development \
favorable traits based on the environment created. From these explorations, \
he uses data on his simulations to draw conclusions and provide insights into \
how these simulations can explain not only the behavior of organisms in the \
natural world, but also by extension us humans. One interesting area that ",
 StyleBox["Primer",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " does not cover in his explorations is the reasons why organisms reproduce \
the way they do, and how they take care of their young--reproductive and \
parenting behaviors. \n\tWhile watching ",
 StyleBox["Primer\[CloseCurlyQuote]s",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " videos, we found his application of Evolutionary Game Theory to discover \
Evolutionarily Stable Strategies a powerful model for breaking down the \
decisions that organisms make on a level that they can be simulated in ABM \
and explored. We would like to apply Agent-Based Modeling and Evolutionary \
Game Theory in Wolfram Language to create a testing ground for investigating \
the emergence of these reproductive and parenthood strategies in organisms. \
Specifically, we imagine creating Wolfram Functions for the Wolfram Community \
so ourselves and users can run experiments on custom models we create, in \
which ourselves and the Wolfram Community can tweak environment setups, agent \
behavior, and other parameters of our model to analyze statistical data \
produced by the simulation and gain insights on reproductive and parenting \
strategies of organisms in the real world.\n\tWe\[CloseCurlyQuote]re \
interested in answering deeper questions through ABM, including but not \
limited to:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->CompressedData["
1:eJwlz30o4wEYB/Cl1VFcE3HUxDUpzahz+ElxNZm7yWi75ojSXJY5uptsOjqi
7o+dO8tdI2Yx43CRd81bUaTRlJyXXUfmzMKtWbeLkd/354+nT9/nn+f7RBRX
5JZ40Wi0SHKgIrhItdZ9/kxe33wvU3G8Tcp6qrXBVtlly09SvoithvH+P7qg
ZumFDrIE0TNQ+JswwlktI2mH9H9jLAEbP27yoK7v9iUUfC2rhMKBBkqrklMH
zZx5FTzbM7ZAyeMVDezo4Q3D/ijTKPzwJ2UDVn6+uoAHsiHvXdxjPvChVGc8
hPwVaRCVJetMaGMYY6GH1fMExgmSc+EOES+G3AXHa0gzTMmgOPHoDRSK9e/2
SRnuNi3UiaIMUHEl+g7NOukwdHxKGIemfuc0tZ9PPIGlPl9c8KDKxrKQBi7/
48BwwpoJZXlDe05S1Y39F6RnTZ3C1vQBO9wa/PYXyi35LshdK7yGYlfMLXwf
skq/RC+lmg81aZM50OwJewXlDr0ErjZll8OOscK3cKQ6VQsli53dkB1AN0Ah
4WeB3nPPKS01BYfwUW+7FS57Jq6hb6jRy0XKi3HX3uBvfVgdvANZNGNj
  "],ExpressionUUID->"e0e41088-64d5-d64b-862a-7a8846769a8c"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Why did natural selection cause organisms to reproduce the way they do (more \
children vs. less children, sexual vs. asexual vs. both (facultative \
parthenogenesis), etc.) and how is this shaped by their environment (less \
food, more predators, etc.)? Finally, how do these behaviors emerge from \
evolution and natural selection? How long do they take to? Is it faster if we \
change the environment?\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977747779895664*^9, 3.977747909970545*^9}, {
   3.9777479760453033`*^9, 3.977747996319334*^9}, {3.9782846698053703`*^9, 
   3.978284683703306*^9}, 3.9782851558552*^9, 
   3.9782852292068596`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"0da7c77e-2393-1b4f-8514-\
8f69cfe01993"],

Cell["\<\
Which behaviors are stronger, and when? Having more children when there is \
more food?\
\>", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977747779895664*^9, 3.977747909970545*^9}, {
  3.9777479760453033`*^9, 
  3.97774801009548*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"39aac43f-c293-f541-87b7-\
520a13abdd06"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
How did the process of parenthood emerge? Why do some organisms abandon their \
young while others guard them preciously? Why do males leave their children \
while females remain with them in some species? How does this relate to their \
reproductive strategy and their environment? \
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977747779895664*^9, 3.977747973199991*^9}, {
   3.9777480142954845`*^9, 3.9777480142954845`*^9}, {3.977748275221031*^9, 
   3.977748275773945*^9}, 
   3.9782852025982533`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"8b340c47-3801-0841-a5d4-\
a218430ff308"],

Cell["\<\
Which parenting strategies are the strongest? Have many offspring and abandon \
them? Guard them preciously? Feed them, but only a little bit, saving most \
resources for yourself? What if there are predators, what if there aren\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t?\
\>", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977747779895664*^9, 3.977747973199991*^9}, {
  3.9777480147378807`*^9, 
  3.977748058903963*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"08c25739-eea4-7842-9c59-\
ab7f91f946f4"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Rationale", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777491654802113`*^9, 3.977749170539337*^9}, {
  3.977750444310425*^9, 
  3.9777504449990673`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"49b35a96-3ef8-bd41-8631-\
ba725d378bc8"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\tIn ecological observational studies, reproductive and parenting behaviors \
of species are analyzed through field studies, mark-recapture population \
counting methods, monitoring habitats, all to take metrics like age at first \
reproduction, broods per female, parental care behaviors, and survivorship \
data. More mathematical analysis is done, and sometimes populations are \
studied directly in labs. In general, the way that species make reproductive \
decisions and care for their young has a complex relationship with \
environmental factors, and such a rationale for these behaviors is thus \
expensive and time-consuming to study since these behaviors depend on \
long-term fitness consequences and nonlinear ecological factors, requiring \
extended observation or experimentation across variable environments. They \
are nonetheless insightful.\t\n\tA simple mathematical model that explains \
the population growth of organisms based on their environment is the logistic \
model for population growth: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox["dP", "dt"], "=", 
    RowBox[{"rP", "(", 
     RowBox[{"1", "-", 
      FractionBox["P", "K"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "800074fb-fca4-2a47-a555-ac69b6b64a92"],
 ", where P is the population at a given time, r is the intrinsic population \
growth rate--the children per-capita per unit time assuming abundant \
resources--and K is the carrying capacity of the environment. This model \
creates unconstrained growth proportional to P when P << K (the more \
organisms, the more children proportional to the number of organisms) but \
factors in the environmental constraint of competition for resources, scaling \
growth such that it becomes 0  when P \[Rule] K.\n\tThis model lends itself \
to the ecological model of r/K selection theory. In r/K selection theory, \
natural selection pressures are hypothesized to drive reproduction strategies \
generally towards r-selection or K-selection. r-selection is indicative of \
high population growth rates from very high amounts of offspring with low \
parental care and Type III survivorship (low probabilities of surviving to \
adulthood). In other words, these are organisms that have more offspring and \
provide less parental care as part of their reproductive strategy, with sheer \
volume mathematically compensating for the fact that offspring receive little \
care. Some of them are bound to survive. Such species with this strategy are \
termed r-selected since they tend to perform well when a population is not \
near carrying capacity (when the rP factor is at its strongest), since their \
rapid reproduction naturally leads to brutal competition near carrying \
capacity. Examples of such organisms are insects and rodents. K-selection is \
indicative of low population growth rates from low amounts of offspring with \
high parental care and Type I survivorship (high probabilities of surviving \
to adulthood). These are organisms that have less offspring but care for them \
more, investing in them to ensure they can reach adulthood. Such species with \
this strategy are termed K-selected since their populations do not \
destabilize like r-selected species near carrying capacity. Humans and eagles \
are examples of K-selected species.\n\tAlthough r/K selection theory offers a \
simple model to explain some organisms\[CloseCurlyQuote] reproductive \
behaviors, other organisms often exhibit combinations of r- and K-selected \
behavior, and r/K selection theory greatly simplifies the rationale for \
reproductive behavior and the longevity of species\[CloseCurlyQuote] \
lifetimes. The model of logistic population growth and r/K selection theory \
piqued our interest because they offer a starting point for those very deep \
questions we asked before. Like these, much biological theory exists and \
explains reproductive and parenting strategies. However, we hope to use \
Agent-Based Modeling to create Wolfram tools with which to explore parenting \
and reproductive behaviors free of the limitations of real world where \
ourselves and our users can make the simulation their own. Here, we can \
observe these behaviors emerge naturally instead of viewing it through the \
lens of pre-established biological theory, and analyze not only what causes \
these parenting and reproductive behaviors, but also the nature of their \
emergence and how they are shaped by rapidly changing environmental factors."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977748092062847*^9, 3.977748094478117*^9}, {
  3.977748132667576*^9, 3.977748165173542*^9}, {3.9777482945682697`*^9, 
  3.9777483080071316`*^9}, {3.9777488166848927`*^9, 3.977748914999687*^9}, {
  3.977749189537657*^9, 3.977749189842188*^9}, {3.9777504877692223`*^9, 
  3.977750488090275*^9}, {3.9782727334800873`*^9, 
  3.978272734182081*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a39c3e64-d141-0a4f-a42c-\
eff48a142729"]
}, Open  ]],

