Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Simulation Goal", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977749951729603*^9, 
  3.9777499527552357`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"685a71fc-609c-2b43-a1d6-\
84f6d9a90f13"],

Cell["\<\
\tAs stated earlier, we hope to create a full fledged Agent-Based Model where \
we may tweak parameters and collect data on the simulation over time. The \
simulation would be complete with visuals and animations, showing what\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s going on behind the data collection. On a higher-level, \
it is important to note that agents do not \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]feel\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] or are not provided \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]rewards\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] by the simulation. Rather, natural selection drives \
some agents to do better than others, causing other agents to die out, \
showing how some strategies are advantageous.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977749958358597*^9, 3.977750017301708*^9}, {
   3.9777501512550735`*^9, 3.9777501674649696`*^9}, 3.977750885979969*^9, {
   3.977753483387802*^9, 
   3.9777535391122284`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ac0f2e2f-663c-fc42-b65a-\
0682e6f194e7"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Agent-Based Model", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777501786406345`*^9, 
  3.9777501825778065`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"fa9ff3b8-c88f-5646-b5ca-\
28b43de1e239"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Agents", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777505613562565`*^9, 
  3.9777505630862675`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"7174897e-a64d-5743-8367-\
8ece14c230ea"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\tOur agents would be stand-ins for whatever organism one would hope to \
emulate in our simulation. They will be governed by simple rules. However, as \
is the case with ABM, their collective behavior ",
 StyleBox["synergizes",
  IgnoreSpellCheck->True],
 " into emergent complexity. \n\tAgents will have 2 vital statistics: age and \
energy. age will be measured in seconds or a certain timestep of our \
simulation and a distinction will be made between two age groups: adult and \
child. There will be a threshold for what age qualifies an agent as an adult \
or a child. Children will inherently have higher energy costs, slower speed, \
or some sort of limitation to simulated the natural vulnerability of \
children. The vulnerability of children can be tweaked as a parameter, and \
will probably influence parenting behavior. Energy is a measure of the \
resources an agent has available to them while they exist in the simulation.\n\
\tAgents will require energy to survive. Agents will only be able to gain \
energy by either 2 ways: consuming food, or receiving energy from a parent. \
Agents may only gain energy in the second way if they are a child.\n\tAgents \
can be created through the process of reproduction between other agents \
(enumerated later on) and they vanish from the environment (die) when they \
either: 1) run out of energy, or 2) exhaust their lifespan, which can be a \
parameter that is set by the user. Parameters are also explained later on.\n\t\
There will be a multitude of ways that Agents will be able to spend their \
energy. These include:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977750573044382*^9, 3.9777508236868725`*^9}, {
  3.9777508629000187`*^9, 3.9777510612759743`*^9}, {3.9777514994778137`*^9, 
  3.977751609816883*^9}, {3.977751652551483*^9, 3.977751712495121*^9}, {
  3.977751753488367*^9, 3.9777518035212307`*^9}, {3.9777527412074394`*^9, 
  3.9777527852947636`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"87343269-ab67-7d44-babb-\
1986530f7058"],

Cell["\<\
Simply existing. Energy will be consumed with time to simulate the bodily \
functions of an organism.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777510637428913`*^9, 3.977751247920191*^9}, {
  3.9777516164607944`*^9, 3.977751617381052*^9}, {3.977751716928919*^9, 
  3.977751731407831*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d2e9baea-fa85-404a-99a3-\
a6aae8853294"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Moving around in the environment. A common way to model energy costs for \
movement in ABM is: energy ~ ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox["speed", "2"], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "49021f87-7174-f14d-b1e6-d00d5944d705"],
 ". This models the conversion of an organisms chemical energy in organisms\
\[CloseCurlyQuote] muscles to kinetic energy--k = ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    FractionBox["1", "2"], 
    SuperscriptBox["mv", "2"]}], TraditionalForm]],
  FormatType->TraditionalForm,ExpressionUUID->
  "c6fd1133-c05f-f042-8019-b2e60596ab87"],
 ". We can either choose our agents to have variable speed or fix their speed \
to be constant, in which case their energy cost for movement will simply be \
constant."
}], "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777510637428913`*^9, 3.977751247920191*^9}, {
  3.9777516164607944`*^9, 
  3.977751617381052*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5dc9a0c9-faa0-1e44-9db6-\
8c7b6e2b2e2b"],

Cell["\<\
Obtaining more food. Such a task will require less energy than an agent gains \
from obtaining food. This amount of energy may also be a simulation parameter.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777510637428913`*^9, 3.9777510862874317`*^9}, {
  3.9777512534470615`*^9, 
  3.9777512749010696`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"11e8c2ce-5d01-104d-96a2-\
6e7082d74bd5"],

Cell["\<\
Reproducing. We can make the amount of energy to reproduce be proportionally \
scale with the amount of offspring, or have it be concave down (having \
triplets seems like it could be more than 3 times as hard as just having 1 \
child). The reproduction energy function can also be a parameter.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777510637428913`*^9, 3.9777510862874317`*^9}, {
  3.9777512534470615`*^9, 3.977751308920908*^9}, {3.977751352684641*^9, 
  3.977751451656925*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6a78cf3d-3bf6-8b42-a7f8-\
0d2dd52f422f"],

Cell["\<\
Parenting. Some agents, by their genes, will be compelled to donate energy to \
their children. This circumvents the process of their children having to find \
their own food, which may be seen as evolutionarily advantageous in some \
environments. This however, comes at a cost to parents and could inhibit \
their own ability to reproduce more or \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]protect\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] their children. A great analogy for the \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]parenting\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] process we hope to \
model is the process by which birds regurgitate digested food to their young.\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777510637428913`*^9, 3.9777510862874317`*^9}, {
  3.9777512534470615`*^9, 3.977751308920908*^9}, {3.977751352684641*^9, 
  3.97775149296274*^9}, {3.977751827258793*^9, 
  3.977751953094837*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c4a701d3-18ca-9c4b-9507-\
89166ccb45aa"],

Cell["\<\
\tNatural instinct will guide the decisions of agents. Agents will obviously \
spend most of their time looking for food. One way this natural instinct is \
simulated is done by just having them randomly move around and if they come \
across food they obtain it. Another way is by just having them have a sensing \
distance and take shortest paths to the nearest food or the nearest mate when \
they are within their sensing distance. How many children agents choose to \
have and how they choose to parent those children will be in their genes, \
which we will define further. But in general, the decisions of an agent will \
deterministic and based off their simulated \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]genes.\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] An example: an agent has the genes: (have one child, \
do not feed and abandon children) will always do those things as such.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777521179721413`*^9, 3.9777522055900135`*^9}, {
   3.9777522464097767`*^9, 3.9777522621293526`*^9}, {3.9777523019047375`*^9, 
   3.9777524340801373`*^9}, {3.977754033922886*^9, 3.9777540390521355`*^9}, 
   3.9777540927721043`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"1086e55a-c9b6-cf4a-b188-\
d4bd94474347"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Predator Agents", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777520119162807`*^9, 
  3.9777520270198936`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e8033681-13fb-e940-b889-\
0623ae64b7f9"],

Cell["\<\
\tIf we find it fruitful, we may introduce an additional layer of complexity \
by putting predators in our simulation that feed on agents. This could add a \
parental dynamic of \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]protecting\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
young and could be an interesting direction to explore. Predator agents would \
have similar movement and consumption rules. However for now, we will not \
implement them and the limiting factor will be resources and competition from \
other agents rather than the threat of predator agents.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977752030451727*^9, 3.9777521110612545`*^9}, 
   3.977754095249096*^9},ExpressionUUID->"747835fa-8ba7-df44-bfec-\
030551143602"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Environment", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777524678526306`*^9, 
  3.9777524687984066`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d5659be7-f23c-3b42-a325-\
aa7e2499a92d"],

Cell["\<\
\tThe environment will have simple rules like days pass, and at the start of \
each day, agents set out from their homes and at the end of each day, agents \
are teleported back to their homes. This circumvents the need for a mechanic \
for where and how agents choose to live, while also simulating a natural \
cycle of the whereabouts of agents. Food can be placed randomly in the \
environment, and its abundance and distribution can be changed as a \
parameter. The food will simulate primary producers in an ecosystem, where \
just as plants produce energy from the seemingly infinite energy of the sun, \
the food will be placed by without fail, at the start of every day (or other \
timestep) as per rules of the simulation. To simulate seasons or resource \
disruptions, we could have the amount of food periodically change (food \
decreases during simulated \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]winter,\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] or food suddenly drops during simulated \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]blight\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote]). Who knows what these \
environmental disruptions will produce. Perhaps parents who don\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]t feed their children when food is abundant go on fine and \
pass their genes since food is so abundant that even children can obtain it. \
Those genes will likely die out in \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]winter\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] situations, as the kids are not fed, where gene \
pools that actually nourish their children will survive and pass on their \
genes.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777524824515476`*^9, 3.9777527310671597`*^9}, {
   3.977752792948433*^9, 3.97775283976939*^9}, {3.977752872844822*^9, 
   3.9777528738166275`*^9}, 
   3.977754096431595*^9},ExpressionUUID->"ee3213e0-6870-024c-910e-\
7f6c4db4a4ab"]
}, Open  ]],

