Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Visuals", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777532120917034`*^9, 
  3.9777532294148808`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"17143292-b851-5e45-8bc2-\
e57a92470a80"],

Cell["\<\
\tWe imagine that agents can be some sort of \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]blob\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] looking thing or just perfect spheres (assume this \
cow is a perfect sphere!). Food could look like an apple or also just be a \
little blob. Child agents will be visually smaller than adult agents. \
Features of agents, like beards or color can also be added to show vital \
signs: energy/age. We\[CloseCurlyQuote]re also imagining energy bars over \
agents. Two agents that are mating can both turn a designated mating color \
indicating that they are mating for visual purposes.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777532365458565`*^9, 3.9777533395776634`*^9}, 
   3.9777541005671673`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"591b5bc4-6b92-cf41-b33a-\
2788f3497080"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Simulation Rules", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977753592186861*^9, 
  3.9777535940018673`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f00c7bd0-d8e8-7147-b482-\
6f4bd0bd4255"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Reproduction", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777536105429287`*^9, 3.97775361120467*^9}, {
  3.977753663044018*^9, 
  3.977753665943041*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"91e03789-d043-ce4f-bf61-\
d45f9f7bca55"],

Cell["\<\
\tWhen two agents have excess energy (will be determined through a \
threshold), they will, rather than seeking food, seek mates, and upon finding \
a mate, reproduce with the mate. This is assuming sexual reproduction in our \
simulation, and we\[CloseCurlyQuote]re still torn between implementing our \
ABM through sexual or asexual reproduction. For now we\[CloseCurlyQuote]re \
assuming sexual. This reproduction will cost energy as explained above, and \
their genes will tell them how many children they should have, costing an \
amount of energy per the reproduction energy function. We have \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]alleles\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] that provide the \
evolutionary behavior and decision making to our agents, and when two mates \
reproduce their alleles will be mixed among their children either through \
haploid mixing. As for the biology don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t worry too much, as \
it will be a simplistic system like this:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777536360950775`*^9, 3.9777536600167046`*^9}, {
   3.97775369707493*^9, 3.977753913075117*^9}, 3.977754102009121*^9, 
   3.9777544306225624`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"9a005540-18b7-9940-a11b-\
4abc4b6de22d"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Agents genetic information consists of two genes:", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977753930160137*^9, 
  3.977753947834734*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"de1eea90-0575-fa43-ab46-\
f842c965eb82"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Gene 1: Reproductive behavior", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977753930160137*^9, 
  3.977753957383644*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d0d50a5b-c95c-f94d-8279-\
5b626d7d2918"],

Cell["\<\
Consists of 1 of many alleles telling the agent how many children to have \
when they reproduce.\
\>", "Subsubitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977753930160137*^9, 3.977753987186596*^9}, {
  3.977754442003807*^9, 
  3.97775444540843*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"eef63250-7274-4045-9cef-\
2a662064076f"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Gene 2: Parenting behavior", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977753930160137*^9, 
  3.977753981902149*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b872775c-74ec-574e-8d9a-\
591a1faba1b8"],

Cell["\<\
Consists of 1 of many alleles telling the agent in what situations to feed \
their children.\
\>", "Subsubitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977753930160137*^9, 3.977753996674383*^9}, {
  3.9777540448662357`*^9, 3.977754044867235*^9}, {3.97775444840432*^9, 
  3.977754450324724*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"ccaec08f-a548-2f45-8de2-\
337e39d742ad"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Child genetic information:", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777541275762444`*^9, 3.9777541353134556`*^9}, 
   3.9777544891665287`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"7ad0e6c0-147f-1445-970b-\
a021255d3be1"],

Cell["\<\
Gene 1: Reproductive Behavior -- Choose randomly between first parent and \
second parent\[CloseCurlyQuote]s allele\
\>", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777541275762444`*^9, 
  3.977754155278471*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3dd1f451-0bc7-a147-8a53-\
0b32cda95bdc"],

Cell["\<\
Gene 2: Parenting Behavior -- Choose randomly between first parent and second \
parent\[CloseCurlyQuote]s allele\
\>", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777541275762444`*^9, 
  3.9777541674959774`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"be82ca47-c32a-454b-bfdf-\
0e4952e3050c"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["This is called haploid mixing.", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777541275762444`*^9, 
  3.9777541722636642`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1d036bc2-5f25-1348-963b-\
5f019ac3bb31"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Parenting", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777542032719746`*^9, 
  3.9777542066556873`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4f571003-7de0-ec4d-92d1-\
ffc4c3f2517c"],

Cell["\<\
\tParenting behavior as explained earlier comes from the second agent gene. \
Agents that have instructions to abandon their children and not feed them \
will not follow their children upon their birth. Agents that have \
instructions to care for their children will travel with them, and donate \
energy to them. The exact mechanics of parenting aren\[CloseCurlyQuote]t yet \
fleshed out, but different parenting strategies are described in the \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]Parenting Strategies\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \
subsection.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777542125971317`*^9, 3.9777543200278625`*^9}, {
  3.9777543502894287`*^9, 
  3.977754369066242*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a9216b98-58f3-fd47-96ee-\
2c876c8b8d3d"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

